User Login vs. Server Login (Machine-to-Machine) in OAuth 2.0

Below is a clean, side-by-side breakdown of the two scenarios, followed by a consolidated differences table.

---

1. The Actors Involved

Actor User Login (Authorization Code + PKCE) Server Login (Client Credentials)
You (the human) ✅ Present — logs in, grants consent ❌ Absent — no human involved
Your Application ✅ Acts on your behalf ✅ Acts on its own behalf
Authorization Server ✅ Issues tokens after user consent ✅ Issues tokens after client authentication
Resource Server (API) ✅ Receives access token, returns your data ✅ Receives access token, returns app-scoped data
Browser / Redirect ✅ Required for login + consent ❌ Never used — direct back-channel only

---

2. Step-by-Step Flow Comparison

Step User Login Server Login
1. Initiation User clicks "Login" in the app App decides it needs an API token (e.g., on startup or on expiry)
2. Identity proof App redirects user's browser to Authorization Server login page App sends client_id + client_secret directly to /token endpoint
3. Consent User sees consent screen and clicks Allow No consent — permissions are pre-configured at client registration
4. Credential returned to app Authorization Server redirects back with a short-lived Authorization Code — (skipped; no intermediate code)
5. Token exchange App's backend exchanges the code + code_verifier (+ client secret) at /token App's backend sends grant_type=client_credentials + credentials at /token
6. Tokens received Access Token + Refresh Token Access Token only (❌ no refresh token)
7. API call App calls Resource Server with Authorization: Bearer <access_token> App calls Resource Server with Authorization: Bearer <access_token>
8. Token expiry Access token expires (e.g., 1 hr) Access token expires (e.g., 5–60 min)
9. Renewal App sends Refresh Token to /token (grant_type=refresh_token) → gets new Access Token + rotated Refresh Token App repeats Step 2 (re-sends client_id + client_secret) → gets new Access Token
10. User involvement on renewal ❌ None — silent, seamless ❌ None — no user exists

---

3. Key Differences (Consolidated)

Dimension User Login (Auth Code + PKCE) Server Login (Client Credentials)
Grant type authorization_code client_credentials
Who is authenticated? The user (and the client) Only the client (application)
Whose data is accessed? The user's resources The application's own resources / service-to-service data
Human interaction Required once (login + consent) Never
Browser redirect Required Not used
Refresh token issued? ✅ Yes ❌ No
How to renew Use the Refresh Token Re-authenticate with client credentials
Client secret required? Yes (for confidential clients) ✅ Yes — always (or private-key JWT)
PKCE (code_verifier) ✅ Required ❌ Not applicable
Consent screen ✅ Shown to user ❌ Never shown
Token lifetime (typical) Access: ~1 hr · Refresh: days–weeks Access: 5–60 min · Refresh: N/A
Best suited for Web apps, mobile apps, SPAs, CLIs acting for a user Backend services, cron jobs, daemons, microservices, CI/CD pipelines
Attack surface if secret leaks Refresh token can be rotated/revoked; user can re-login Client secret is the master key — must be rotated immediately
RFC reference RFC 6749 §4.1 + RFC 7636 (PKCE) RFC 6749 §4.4

---

4. One-Line Mental Model

 Mental Model
User Login "I am acting for Alice — prove Alice said yes, then give me a key to her data."
Server Login "I am acting as myself — here are my credentials, give me a key to the API."

---

5. Decision Rule

Ask: "Whose data am I accessing?"

· If it's a specific user's data → User Login (Authorization Code + PKCE)
· If it's no particular user's data (the app's own data, or a shared service account) → Server Login (Client Credentials)

That single question determines the entire flow, token strategy, and security posture.